WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.120
Although the Sandin family seems kind of neutral
and doesn’t participate in The Purge themselves,

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.160
if we read into the subtext we can see how
they really feel.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.530
The display of blue baptisias is a sign of
support for The Purge in the same way that

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.220
a pink ribbon is a sign of support for the
fight against breast cancer in our world.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.090
This light blue to cyan color can be found
all over the Sandin’s house, it’s on their

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.840
curtains, their kitchen, their furniture,
their possessions and even their clothes.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.103
All of the neighbors who participate in The
Purge also don this color.

00:00:25.103 --> 00:00:29.504
Charlie, however, is fundamentally against the Purge and he is the only one not to wear the color.

00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:34.050
Even the interface of their security system
uses this color of blue, and this is the system

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.050
that is supposed to keep them safe and keep
the homeless out on the street to be purged.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.570
The Sandins do not initially have bad intentions,
but it is because of The Purge that Mr. Sandin

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.960
is able to sell a ton of security systems
and become rich.

00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:50.399
“Ten years ago, we could barely afford rent.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:52.570
Now we're thinking about buying a boat.”

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:56.990
So the financial comfort caused by The Purge
causes Mr. and Mrs. Sandin to unknowingly

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.149
look past how bad it really is.

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:02.960
The baptisia blue color also becomes a symbol
of the political party behind The Purge,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.938
the New Founding Fathers of America, or the
NFFA.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.539
In a television interview early on, the behavioral
scientist Dr. Peter Buynak also wears this

00:01:10.539 --> 00:01:14.770
color as he explains how The Purge is effective,
claiming that it creates psychological stability

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.159
by allowing pent up aggression to be released.

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:20.859
However, later sequels would confirm that
this data isn’t accurate and it’s actually

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:21.859
quite the opposite.

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:26.289
So the baptisia blue he is wearing is likely
a clue to the audience that this man is an

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:29.540
NFFA plant spreading misinformation for political
reasons.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.799
I’ll also explore how one of the neighbors
has ties to a high ranking NFFA official,

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:36.423
so stick around to the end of this video to
hear about it.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:41.649
America.

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:43.299
2022.

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:45.039
Unemployment is at 1%.

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:48.389
Yeahh… doesn’t look like we’re gonna
make that in real life.

00:01:48.389 --> 00:01:49.529
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:01:49.529 --> 00:01:53.950
Today I’m covering The Purge -- the dystopian
not-too-distant future horror story about

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.679
a night each year where all crime is legal
for 12 continuous hours.

00:01:57.679 --> 00:02:01.469
In the opening credits we see video feeds
of the previous nationwide Purges that have

00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:04.219
taken place for the past 6 years leading up
to this night.

00:02:04.219 --> 00:02:08.429
The story is just a small snapshot of one
family’s experience in this messed up version

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.319
of society, but it’s simplicity is also
what makes it great, and it still contains

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:16.340
setups to the greater lore that we would become
familiar with as the franchise would expand.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.692
So let's lower the security doors and take
a deeper look into some of those hidden details.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.220
As James Sandin returns home from work, he
hears a guy on the radio explaining his own

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:31.640
plans for Purge night.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.040
“Pete from Northern Virginia, what’s your
Purge plan?”

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.703
“I’m gonna hunt down my boss.

00:02:35.756 --> 00:02:37.239
That son of a ***** has it coming.”

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:41.560
The idea of an employee going after his or
her boss would be explored in further detail

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.980
in The Purge Season 1.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.646
And I don’t want to already be going on
this rant, but I’m already going on this rant.

00:02:46.646 --> 00:02:49.019
It’s so annoying how the Purge franchise
is titled.

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:53.610
If I say “The Purge”, I could be referring
to this original movie, the TV series called

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.610
The Purge or the franchise as a whole.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.310
But I can’t say “The First Purge” because
the fourth movie is called The First Purge.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.860
They should have called the prequel The Purge:
Experiment and they should have given each

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.969
season of the series a subtitle, like The
Purge: Homecoming and The Purge: Bloodstained

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:08.450
Saints or something like that.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.569
Those are actually pretty good.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:11.349
We soon meet the Sandin kids.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:13.660
Zoe is the bratty teen prep student with a
ton of accolades.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.760
She’s an excellent writer, softball player
and gymnast.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.471
She even won a marathon and is also an excellent
horseback rider.

00:03:19.471 --> 00:03:22.109
Her little brother is very much the opposite.

00:03:22.109 --> 00:03:25.580
He’s more of a science standout, engineering
his own drone that he calls Timmy.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.189
I’m not sure if this is an intentional Toy
Story reference, but it is very similar to

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:31.560
Sid’s babyface creation so I’m gonna count
it.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.630
Charlie also has some disturbing drawings
of The Purge hidden in the secret spot behind

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:36.630
his closet.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:40.099
These are never explained, and so we’re
only left to wonder if this seemingly innocent

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:41.650
character has a dark side.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.840
I know the idea of a story about the kid who
once lost a parent in The Purge has already

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.310
been done a couple times now, but it would
at least be interesting to see where Charlie

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.310
and Zoey ended up.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:51.910
They would just get Tommy Wiseau to play Charlie
as an adult.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.349
At dinner, Charlie tells about his day at
school.

00:03:55.012 --> 00:04:00.270
“In English I wrote a story about a man
whose love is so powerful that it can kill

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.560
people so he cuts out his own heart.”

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.730
“That is so sweet.”

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.819
This story is kind of similar to what happens
to Mr. Sandin in The Purge.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:11.239
It is his love for his family that fuels his
motivation to give Dante up to the purgers

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:15.060
so that they can be safe, but he realizes
the error of his ways which ultimately means

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.519
sacrificing his own life to save everyone
else.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:20.920
When he locks down the house, we can see the
security code 101382.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.590
I was looking around online to see if there
might be any significance behind that number.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:30.320
Famousfix.com suggests that “this equates
to the date 10/13/1982, the date of Ronald

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.610
Reagan's Address to the Nation on the Economy.”

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:36.130
I’m somewhat skeptical that this could be
the motivation, but it’s worth noting that

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.140
The Purge is supposed to boost the economy.

00:04:38.225 --> 00:04:39.225
“Crime is down.

00:04:39.225 --> 00:04:41.669
The economy is flourishing.”

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:44.560
The family gets together for lockdown when
James notices this.

00:04:45.822 --> 00:04:46.991
“Hey look at this.

00:04:46.991 --> 00:04:49.509
Mr. Sabian and Mr. Barbero are going hunting.

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:50.509
That’s daring.”

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:52.099
The name Sabian is significant.

00:04:52.099 --> 00:04:55.319
At the time, this was just kind of a throw
away reference that director James Demonaco

00:04:55.319 --> 00:04:56.639
liked to put in his movies.

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:01.229
His 2005 film Assault on Precinct 13 had a
character named Dr. Alex Sabian and he wrote

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:05.569
the screenplay for the 1998 film The Negotiator
which had a character named Chris Sabian.

00:05:05.569 --> 00:05:09.150
These were probably a reference to Andrea
Sabian, who he’s given a special thanks

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.930
to in the credits of The Purge and The Purge:
Election Year.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.310
But looking back on this line now, we can
connect it to a character who was part of

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:16.500
The Purge experiment.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.030
Arlo Sabian was the NFFA Chief of Staff who
tried to manipulate the results of the experiment

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.660
by killing people to make it look like it
had been a success.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.966
It’s hard to say whether or not this could actually be him,

00:05:25.966 --> 00:05:29.550
but it could definitely be a relative, as they both obviously support the
NFFA.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:33.320
Mr. Sandin would spend the beginning of the
evening finishing some paperwork, but another

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.286
secret can be found on the TV he has playing
in the background.

00:05:46.391 --> 00:05:50.260
Zoe goes back to her room, where she discovers
that her boyfriend Henry never left the house

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.260
that afternoon.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.360
I noticed her pillow has text on it that reads:
Lights Out.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.569
This isn’t a reference to the movie Lights
Out, but it could be a foreshadowing of the

00:05:57.569 --> 00:05:59.550
moment that the power gets cut.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.840
As James finishes up his work from that day, listen to the TV in the background.

00:06:03.845 --> 00:06:07.650
“We’re looking at some live feeds from
around the country and discussing The Purge

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.830
with criminologist Tommy Aagard.”

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.690
Tommy Aagard is also a significant name, it’s
a reference to the first assistant editor

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.260
and visual effects editor.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.390
One of the news broadcasts features Purge
coverage of Staten Island.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.270
Director James Demonaco is kind of obsessed
with Staten Island.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:24.879
His first movie was originally titled Staten
Island before the title was changed to Little

00:06:24.879 --> 00:06:25.879
New York.

00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:28.920
He also wrote and produced The First Purge,
which was set on Staten Island, and he’s

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.750
working on a new movie called Once Upon a
Time in Staten Island.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.690
I told you -- he’s obsessed.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:37.460
Charlie feels bad when he sees a homeless
man, who we later find out to be named Dante

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.979
Bishop, begging for help on the streets and
temporarily disarms the security system to

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:45.280
let him inside, then right as that happens
Henry attacks Mr. Sandin, who had been against

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.039
Henry’s relationship with his daughter.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:49.280
This allows Dante to escape into the house.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.189
Mr. Sandin fires back at Henry, which proves
to be a fatal reaction.

00:06:52.189 --> 00:06:56.099
A group of purgers outside threaten to tear
down the door if they don’t deliver Dante,

00:06:56.099 --> 00:06:59.870
so Mr. Sandin goes to look for him, and in
the hall he comes across his family photos

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.810
covered in blood - a symbol of his worst case
scenario for Purge night.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:07.660
Eventually he finds Dante, but he’s taken
Zoe as a hostage, and this scene is part of

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.509
what makes this Purge movie the best.

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:13.590
There are lots of scenes in the other movies
where suspense is artificially created because

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:17.150
a purger holding someone at gunpoint just doesn’t
pull the trigger when they should.

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:19.090
Sometimes to a ridiculous extent.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:21.130
This scene, however, has a reason for it.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:25.050
If Dante kills Zoe, there’s no reason for
James and Mary not to jump him and give him

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.050
to the purgers.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.161
Mrs. Sandin sneaks up behind him, but she
won’t shoot him either, because if they

00:07:29.161 --> 00:07:32.900
can’t turn Dante over to the purgers alive,
they’ll break into the house and release

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.699
the beast on the family instead.

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:36.470
Mr. Sandin has to make a moral decision.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:41.169
Does he kill a possibly innocent man to guarantee
his own family’s safety, or take his chances

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:44.039
against them, perhaps putting everyone’s
life in danger?

00:07:44.039 --> 00:07:47.169
Ultimately, he ends up feeling empathy for
Dante, perhaps due to him being able to feel

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:50.650
sympathy for his situation because he was
poor just 10 years ago.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:54.530
James doesn’t make it, but his decision
still ends up saving his family, because Dante

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:57.750
returns the favor by protecting them when
the neighbors turn against them later that

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:58.250
night.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:02.060
During the end credits, we learn that this
was supposedly the most “successful” Purge

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:05.748
yet according to the NFFA who just want to
see a lot of violence and weapon sales.

00:08:05.748 --> 00:08:10.523
"The New Founding Fathers have released an early statement saying this was the most successful Purge yet."

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:15.039
This is a running theme in the franchise -- it
seems like each Purge is always more violent

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:16.039
than the last.

00:08:16.039 --> 00:08:19.980
The next Purge would take place one year later,
and feature many more families struggling

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.250
to stay alive during the annual holiday.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:26.319
So click that video on the left and join me
in the next episode of Things You Missed where

00:08:26.319 --> 00:08:28.590
I’ll be covering The Purge: Anarchy.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.690
If you love horror, remember to subscribe
to CZsWorld for new horrors every week, ring

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:36.800
the deathbell for notifications and I’ll
see you in the next one.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.120
Assuming we both survive.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.670
And like The Purge, I recommend you stay home,
but if you do have to go out: wear a mask.

